## --- Async namespace (sub-phase 4A B1) -----------------------------
##
## The user-facing async runtime surface. `Async.sleep(d)` returns a
## TimeSleepFuture that resolves after `d` has elapsed; usable from
## both sync code via `block_on(Async.sleep(d))` and async code via
## `Async.sleep(d).await` (per async_io.spec.md B-Z).
##
## Why a class rather than a free fn: class-static-method dispatch
## is unambiguous about the async runtime surface (cf. discussion
## around naming — sync stays on `Thread.sleep`, async lives on
## `Async.*`). Future additions land here as more class-statics.
class Async
  def self.sleep(d: &Duration) -> TimeSleepFuture
    TimeSleepFuture.new(d)
  end
end

## --- Sub-phase 5: task scheduler (commit 1) ------------------------
##
## Spec: docs/specs/stdlib/task_spawn.spec.md.
##
## Commit 1 ships the queue + spawn + yield_now + raw handle
## accessors. Commit 2 layers `task_join(h).await` over this surface
## via the existing free-fn `.await` desugar. The TaskHandle is held
## as an opaque `Int` at the Riven surface, mirroring the convention
## established by `JoinHandle.join_raw(handle: Int)` in library/std/
## sync/src/lib.rvn (an OS-thread join handle).
##
## Trade-off documented in the brief: a typed `class TaskHandle[T]`
## value-class would need lib-decl support for `&var Future` /
## `self` ↔ `Int` cast at the call boundary. v1 ships at the raw
## Int level (same as Thread.spawn_raw). Commit 2 introduces a typed
## __TaskJoinFuture[T] that recovers T at the join site.
